{\*\generator Msftedit 5.41.21.2510;}\viewkind4\uc1\pard\sl276\slmult1\lang11\b\i\f0\fs22 Complete PSRC Spritepacks Creation Workflow\b0\i0\par
\par
\ul\b Summary:\par
\ulnone\b0\par
Overall step-by-step, program-by-program workflow pipeline for the Pixel Sprites a la Reactorcore project.\par
\par
It involves drawing MANY sprites on a huge sheet, then choosing how do I pack them into organized packages. Its meant as a mass-production method for creatings hundreds of sprites per chosen theme. \par
\par
I rarely draw anything individually on its own; instead I draw everything all at once at the same time.\par
\par
Its a method that is feasible for a single person to crank out thousands of sprites that are all standardized, modular/swappable/interchangable, reusable, intercompatible and highly flexible, intended to support giant game projects that have the potential for thousands of pieces of content. \par
\par
Each sprite can be repurposed as both an inventory icon or a physical physics-engine enabled object. There's a lot of built-in game design consideration baked into how they're made.\par
\par
This is how I build a new PSRC sprites package from start to end.\par
\par
\ul\b Steps:\ulnone\b0\par
\par
\pard{\pntext\f0 1.\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pnf0\pnindent0\pnstart1\pndec{\pntxta.}}
\fi-360\li720\sl276\slmult1 Choose subject/idea/theme.\par
{\pntext\f0 2.\tab}Start with general megasheet and draw thing according to a larger, global theme (such as torso clothing, hat collection, ballistic guns, energy weapons, fruit n veg, electronics, grenades and explosive, shoes, road signs, etc).\par
{\pntext\f0 3.\tab}Gain a deeper understanding of the theme/subject/idea (research, read, reference).\par
{\pntext\f0 4.\tab}Prepare canvas of comfortable size and give it a soft desatured colored background that is pleasant to look at instead of the default pure white canvas. Prepare reference images for easy access and viewing during drawing, You're essentially sketching the objects from those reference images by eye approximation ("eeh kinda looks like this"). \line\par
\b{\pntext\f0 5.\tab}ABSOLUTELY MAKE SURE YOU ARE SAVING THE IMAGE AS A LOSSLESS IMAGE FORMAT; That means using .PNG format or if you want to keep separate layer data too, then whatever format your program uses (.PSPIMAGE in Paint Shop Pro 9, .PSD in Photoshop, .PND in Paint.NET, .ASE for Asesprite, and so on.) \b0\line\line\ul\b NEVER, EVER SAVE ANY PIXEL ART AS .JPG FILE, IT WILL RUIN YOUR WORK.\ulnone\b0\line\par
{\pntext\f0 6.\tab}Check scale standards (1m = 32px, human 1.85m = 60px). Stay consistent and constantly compare each sprite to human and real measurements where available. Use intuition if data is missing. That said, since PSRC is a hybrid real-cartoon style, make the final object be -20% more compact/condensed/smaller compared to true measurements. While its more accurate to say human is 1.85m hyper-realistically, I'm pretending the human is actually 1.75m tall with their same 60px height.\par
{\pntext\f0 7.\tab}Draw subjects/objects in the order of the reference materials to later keep track of which one was which. I essentially draw objects in batches like a sandwitch factory; first as unshaded shapes/blobs which establish a base model with minimal detail if even that, then later I do a shading pass while also fixing scale/size issues and also making compromises between details I can include and what I skip over for sake of the finished piece.\par
{\pntext\f0 8.\tab}First pass is to draw subjects with 3 shades; middle grey, light grey, dark grey, + black for "holes" and white for "markings". Much later, proper detail and shading/rendering is applied, but not now. This is done to first get the proportions and consistency of all objects right, to match them with the default human character before commiting to drawing them out in final detail. I want to have an array of everything that I can see an overview of it all, that they're correct size and proportions and look ok with the human.\par
{\pntext\f0 9.\tab}Advance in the following stages (steps 9-12) when actually drawing:\par
{\pntext\f0 10.\tab}Model out the initial shape by either drawing the outline using the line art method (for bigger straight things like wooden crates) or by drawing it as a grey blob (both cases use 128 luminance grey as the middle grey).\par
{\pntext\f0 11.\tab}When drawing hard-to-see details on an object, where they might blend due to lack of shading, I would initially color them with bright red-blue colors. Use the color replacer to make them bright/dark/grey/whatever once the area around them is done. Can be useful if drawing complex objects as a technique.\par
{\pntext\f0 12.\tab}Add major details using light grey, dark grey, white and black to "find" the distinctive shapes within the subject (if drawing a rifle like the AK-47, make the body of the gun middle grey, while grip and barrel can be dark grey, and the wooden parts can be bright grey. Use black for vent holes and white to mark where the fire selector would be). This technique helps you build the object in enough detail that it makes sense, even without shading at this stage.\par
{\pntext\f0 13.\tab}Apply natural, neutral, ambient, top-right source shading (I use a 50% opacity burn brush, but you can also use a greyscale palette of 9 shades of grey or more.)\par
{\pntext\f0 14.\tab}Also give a light/subtle atmospheric ambient shading that adds areas of reflected light.\par
{\pntext\f0 15.\tab}Refine the sprites on sheets to their most complete forms (Greyscale)\line (As in: shape, scale and shading is finalized).\par
{\pntext\f0 16.\tab}Extract completed objects to individual smaller sheets by groups/specific theme, try to fix any last moment issues with each object's scale, shape, shading here.\par
{\pntext\f0 17.\tab}Align resulting sprites to sheets: "Optimized" (actual ingame use) and "InfoSheet" (letting the customer see what the pack has, as a showcase).\par
{\pntext\f0 18.\tab}Sort and align the objects largest-to-smallest, along with top-Left_to_bottom-Right order (Unless it makes logical sense to have certain smaller objects before larger ones).\par
{\pntext\f0 19.\tab}Make sure "Optimized" spritesheet's pixel resolution is Power of 2 (64x64px, 128x128px, 256x256px, 512x512px, and so on.)\par
{\pntext\f0 20.\tab}Make "Optimized" sheet have transparent BG.\par
{\pntext\f0 21.\tab}CLEAR ANY ROGUE PIXELS! You can create a new BG layer, flood fill it with neon green (hex color '#00ff00' or 'RGB 0,255,0', then switch to the sprites layer and use the eraser tool to delete any pixels you accidently left out that aren't supposed to be there around the objects. They're otherwise hard to spot against a transparent background, but this "green check" method reveals them quickly and easily. Delete the temporary green background layer afterwards.)\par
{\pntext\f0 22.\tab}Create alternative sheets based of the grey template "Optimized" sheet\line (Colored, Alternative Colored, Darker, Lighter etc. whichever ones you need for your particular sheet. Lets say I'm making a set of cactus plants and I want them to have different coloring styles while the objects themselves stay the same, like winter style colors, tropical colors, wasteland colors, etc.)\par
{\pntext\f0 23.\tab}Recheck shading and CLEAR ANY ROGUE PIXELS AGAIN! Just in case, they might sneak up on you. Again, use the green check method again if you feel like there might be those pesky rogue pixels somewhere in there.\par
{\pntext\f0 24.\tab}Save sheets as "PSRC_PackName_Type.png" (as an example: 'PSRC_CactusPlants_Greyscale.png', 'PSRC_CactusPlants_Tropical.png', 'PSRC_CactusPlants_Winter.png', etc.)\par
{\pntext\f0 25.\tab}Put said sheets into their own folders within 'PSRC Sprites' folder (if you're making a larger collection under a single namespace, where PSRC Sprites is a "master folder" where all your sprite packs live and each individual pack has its own folder like "PSRC CactusPlants" or something like that.)\par
{\pntext\f0 26.\tab}Run through  Alferd Sprite Unpacker/Slicer to produce seperate images from sheets.\par
{\pntext\f0 27.\tab}If its a multipart character, (i.e. dragon, huge snake, wolf, power armor), create a bone structure/rig for it and have some kind of demo files/images to explain to the customer how they should do the assembly of the subject - how they should use it).\par
{\pntext\f0 28.\tab}OPTIONAL: Create special sheets (silhouettes only, wireframe only, stylistic black/white). Can be neat for convenience if the customer prefers ready-to-use styles instead of using their game engine to recolor/restyle the sprites using shaders.\par
\pard\sl276\slmult1\par
\ul\i Final Sheet Names:\par
\ulnone\i0 1. PSRC_PackName_SheetType.png\par
2. PSRC_PackName_SheetType_Subtype.png\par
3. PSRC_PackName#_SheetType_Subtype#.png\par
\par
Ex:\par
1. PSRC_UrbanMaintenance_Infosheet.png\par
2. PSRC_StarterPack_Optimized_Template.png\par
3. PSRC_KitchenGear2_Optimized_Color3.png\par
\par
\par
\ul\i Object Names: \par
\ulnone\b\i0 AFTER USING ALFERD TO CUT SHEET INTO INDIVIDUAL IMAGES, DO NOT RENAME INDIVIDUAL OBJECT SPRITE FILES TO WHAT THEY ARE, JUST LET USER PUT THEIR OWN NAMES OR USE AS IS. SOME SPRITES MAY BE MULTI-USE, INTERPRETED AS MANY DIFFERENT THINGS. NAMING THEM EXPLICITLY COULD BLOCK THIS CREATIVE USE.\par
 \par
NAMING ALSO CAN MESS UP THE SHEET ORDER UNLESS YOU NUMBER THEM WITH A PREFIX. GENERALLY YOUD WANT THE SORTING ORDER TO BE THE SAME FOR INDIVIDUAL OBJECTS AS THEY APPEAR ON THE ORIGINAL SHEET THEY WERE CUT FROM.\par
\par
PUT SLICED SPRITES INTO THEIR OWN SHEET NAME'D SUBFOLDERS. BASICALLY NAME SUBFOLDERS WITH "SHEET_TYPE". (Like "Template", "Color Red", "Tropical", etc.)\par
\par
DEFAULT ORIGINAL GREY 128 LUM VERSIONS IS ALWAYS CALLED "Template" SUBFOLDER.\b0\par
\line\ul\b\par
Ideas for how to organize and name individual sprites:\ulnone\b0\par
\par
\b\i TYPE1:\b0\i0  Subfolder - SheetType\par
*SheetType_001.png\par
\par
\b\i TYPE2:\b0\i0  Name includes subtype\par
*Packname_SheetType_001.png\par
\par
\b\i TYPE3:\b0\i0  Just a number\par
*001.png\par
\par
Use whichever you feel works best for you. Each pack is different and may have different needs/considerations.\par
\par
\ul\b For final result you can use following folder structure:\ulnone\b0\par
\par
\pard\li720\sl276\slmult1 *Pixel Sprites Reactorcore\tab\tab (Global Root folder for all packs)\par
+*Name of theme/object/idea\tab\tab (Subfolder within Root)\par
++Sheets\tab\tab\tab\tab (Optimized, InfoSheet, Specials)\par
++*[sheetname]\tab\tab\tab (For each sheet, individual objects)\par
++*Anims/Rigs folder \tab\tab (animations may be reusable for other objects)\b\par
\b0\par
* = folder\par
+* = subfolder\par
+ = file/folder within this depth level\par
\pard\sl276\slmult1\par
\par
\ul\b Bonus: Settings for sprite image imports in Unity:\ulnone\b0\par
-Type = Sprite (2D/uGUI)\par
-Pixels per Unit = 32 (1m = 32px)\par
-Filter mode = Point (Crispy look)\par
-Format = 16bit\par
\par
\par
\ul\b Aligning objects on the usable optimized spritesheet:\ulnone\b0\par
For optimized sheet, ALWAYS layout the object comic-style Top to Bottom, Left to Right for better auto-ordering by sprite slicer applications. Pay close attention to the top-leftmost pixel, placing it always further to the right or below the previous object's top-leftmost pixel.\par
