\ul\b Coloring phase:\par
\ulnone\b0 I only start coloring after I have my completed, fully optimized greyscale sheets. I never edit the original pixels of the greyscale sprites, I simply make a new copy of the greyscale sprite sheet as a new image, then on it, I draw on a new separate layer that magically applies the color to the greyscale objects without ever damaging them. All by using layer modes.\par
\par
This allows me to recolor, repattern, retexture the object as much as I want. It is one the easiest and most fun parts of the PSRC workflow.\par
\par
\pard{\pntext\f0 1.\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pnf0\pnindent0\pnstart1\pndec{\pntxta.}}
\fi-360\li720\sl276\slmult1 Make sure you're not overwriting the original greyscale image, simply duplicate/copypaste an existing optimized greyscale spritesheet and work on that without needing to worry about the original work being ruined.\par
{\pntext\f0 2.\tab}Create a new raster layer with "Color" name.\par
{\pntext\f0 3.\tab}Apply color to objects ON the new Color Layer, NOT the original grey-scale template.\par
{\pntext\f0 4.\tab}Set the "Color" raster layer to 'Hard Light' mode in the layer settings. Other modes could be 'Color', 'Overlay', 'Screen', 'Multiply', etc. Whichever looks good to you.\par
{\pntext\f0 5.\tab}(Optional) Using the "Curves" tool in PSP9, adjust the luminosity of the objects or color layer to desired levels if the end result seems a bit off regarding shading and readability.\par
{\pntext\f0 6.\tab}Tweak colors & brightness/contrast/curves/gamma until it looks neat/ok.\par
{\pntext\f0 7.\tab}Start with main colors. First use the magic wand tool to select everything that isn't the objects, inverse the selection so that only the objects are selected, now use a paintbrush over them safely to apply major colors as needed using rough brushstrokes/flood fills. Then apply the secondary colors/details to finish the coloring job.\par
{\pntext\f0 8.\tab}Use desaturated/muted 50-70% saturation level colors generally for a nicer realistic-ish look, while attempting to match lightness when picking colors to paint with over the grey template objects.\par
{\pntext\f0 9.\tab}Using the hue tools (Hue,Sat,Lig & Hue Map), you can easily create multiple quick alternative variations of colorings from the first one you painted.\par
\pard\sl276\slmult1\par
