\par
# Human Character\par
Pixel Sprites a la Reactorcore (PSRC)\par
\par
## About\par
Human being pixel art sprites for a modular paper-doll style physics ragdoll character for game development or 2D animation uses.\par
\par
Contains separate torsos, heads, limbs, hands and arms that you can attach and combine how you like in your projects. Comes with several realistic skin tone options.\par
\par
## What is included:\par
+ \b Infosheet -\b0  for reference, overview and information of everything in the pack.\par
+ \b Optimized sheets\b0  - all sprites are crammed into it in the same order as the individual images from top left to bottom right comic style reading order.\par
+ \b Individual images -\b0  these are found in the subfolders if you prefer them this way.\par
\par
## PSRC Standard Scale\par
Remember these universal rules that apply to all PSRC sprites:\par
- 1 Meter = 32 Pixels\par
- 8 pixels = 25cm\par
- Average Human = around 60 pixels tall or 1,75m\par
- Hybrid real-cartoon style, realistic measurements but -20% more compact.\par
- Zoom in up to 600%\par
- Zoom out up to 25%\par
\par
## Detailed PSRC artstyle information\par
PSRC is a comprehensive visual representation system for a 2D sideview game.\par
\par
* Highly flexible; capable of supporting a complete universe simulator super game (sort of like open world sandbox games, but directed). \par
\par
* Easily extendable and modifiable; all assets are provided as greyscale value images that can take on any colors via an overlay layer.\par
\par
* Easy to learn and expand; sprites are drawn according to a straighforward process that anyone can learn.\par
\par
* Not perfect and thats a good thing; anyone that extends the sprite set doesn't have to be exact as long they use the same palette and principles - resulting in varied and rich objects that despite being drawn by different artists with relatively different tools (Paintshop Pro 9, Asesprite, Graphics Gale, Paint.NET, etc), they are all still compatible.\par
\par
* Indie-ready, Modder-friendly, Inclusive collaboration wise - meaning I could easily bring in outside people to work on a project and give them clear instructions to produce quality standardized content that isn't overwhelming or intimidating to grasp.\par
\par
* Uses neutral shading so it can work fine with most lighting system solutions out there.\par
\par
* The human character is effectively a robust custom character system allowing for very rich modular customization, yet still providing sane standards to make designing clothes, hairstyles and other addons managable - both in terms of drawing them and in terms of size/shape categories. They can be used as a physics ragdoll, a pre-rendered set of animation frames, an inverse-kinematic driven rigged 2D paper-doll model, or any combination of the above per game project.\par
\par
* Can be zoomed between 25%-600% size scale and still look great and visually crisp.\par
\par
* Can be combined with lowpoly 2D/3D models or scalable vector shapes (triangles, squares, circles) along with seamlessly tiled texture images and overlay color shaders to serve large or huge objects that are otherwise too big to be drawn by hand. This creates a tandem system that can handle anything from a tiny flower to an entire mountain, or planet or even a massive star. Perfect for an open world universe simulation game that plays like a 2D platformer.\par
