\ul\b Screenshots & Keyimages for Stores:\ulnone\b0\par
Just take the greyscale sheet and any colored versions and create non-stealable release images for them:\par
\par
1. Resize image by 300%, 400%, 500% or 600% (Pixel Resize or Nearest Neighbour, no Anti-alias). Use bigger resizes for smaller pixel resolution sheets, like 128px = 500%, 256px = 400%, etc.\par
2. Rotate image by 5 degrees left (counter-clockwise)\par
3. Resize image by 99% (same, pixel resize too)\par
4. Add a watermark text in one of the corners (Use the text to write your author name)\par
Save as a new JPG image.\par
\par
Done, you now have useful images for store, social media and promotion. You can further crop for zoomed in highlights or different aspect ratios, resize bigger versions (starting from step 1 though) and use them as base/background for promotional text that you can write on top of the image when making an announcement, a sale or a teaser.\par
\par
The result is good because its:\par
- Its large without needing to zoom in\par
- It shows everything clearly\par
- It is easy and quick for me to make, even possible to automate too, done with a single click\par
- It "ruins" the image so it becomes unusable for anyone trying to right-click>save-as to try to "steal" the pixel art package without paying for it or without downloading the official package\par
- Yet despite "ruining it", it still pretty and impressive enough for showing off on the store\par
- Has my author name on it if it gets shared around on the internet\par
\par
\par
\ul\b Multipart Object Mockup Screenshots & Prefab Demos\par
\par
\ulnone\b0 If I'm making a multipart character or a composite thing that is assembled from multiple separate sprites, then I should assemble them into a static mockup so people can see what it might look like when assembled.\par
\par
Create a !Demo folder, which may contain an example of the assembled character. Nothing fancy, not animated or rigged. Just a prefab that shows the final result on how the character is supposed to be put together, how the layers or game object structure are to be sorted and how might the parts be named.\par
\par
Commonly, these three poses could be useful:\par
1. Natural common action pose (walking, posing, running, leaping)\par
2. Stiff idle standing pose (Just standing up straight, nothing special)\par
3. Functional stiff pose (Emphasis on showing all limbs and connections)\par
\par
Using these prefabs, a screenshot of 300-600% magnification is taken and put against a 236 Lum grey background (RGB 236,236,236 or Hex #eeeeee).\par
