\par
\ul\b Art Tips: Shading, Materials\ulnone\b0\par
\par
Shading stuff basically depends on two distinct aspects: Glossiness and Texture.\par
Also the shape/geometry of the object itself, but thats a given.\par
\par
If something is very \ul shiny, glossy\ulnone , then shading will be of \b high-contrast \b0 type.\par
Conversively, if something is \ul dull, flat\ulnone , then shading will be \b low-contrast\b0 .\par
\par
Another aspect is Reflectiveness. This occurs with materials like metal and glass.\par
The whole idea behind reflective materials is reflection. They basically reflect the area(the emitted light) around them on their surface like a mirror. The shape of the reflective object can distort the reflection and curve the light.\par
\par
As a general rule, all objects are to be shaded with a top-right-downwards light (sun) and a dim, atmospheric reflected ambient light. Dull materials are shaded flat, while Glossy materials are shaded strongly with higher contrast.\par
\par
Texture is merely an extension of the shape of the object, but it plays a role in defining the shininess of an object. If the object is rough, it will be dull, but if its clean flat, it will be shiny.\par
But ultimately, texture is just the shape of the object on a micro-level, which defines where shadows and highlights may fall at a tiny scale.\par
\par
Also, as pixel art happens on such a tiny scale, simplification is necessary. The idea is to convert areas/objects of high detail with more arbstract simplified representation of said areas.\par
\par
\par
\ul\b Art Tips: Coloring & Hue Shifting\ulnone\b0\par
\pard\sl240\slmult1\par
Coloring happens by adding a new layer in the mode "Hard Light, Overlay, Screen, Multiply, or Color (Legacy)", then adding the desired color. After the color is added, the greyscale templates underneath are adjusted with the Curves tool (kind of like brightness and contrast tool, but it uses curves to adjust highlights, middles and darks of an image's pixels) to match the luminosity of the desired color and desired final result.\par
\par
As an emulation of reflective/atmospheric light or distance, Hue Shitfing can be used optionally.\par
\par
Basically, there is the main middle color that is the natural color of the object, meanwhile shadows rotate towards the cooler tone (Clockwise -> , red to purple-blue-green) and highlights rotate towards warmer tones (Counter-clockwise <-, red to orange-yellow-green).\par
\par
Personally, this is only applicable on larger sprites that have a supposed, but very insignificant source of light that may illuminate another surface of that same object, causing a hue shift at that location only, because its basically a reflective/additional lightsource.\par
\par
By "insignificant light source", I mean a supposed light source so small or ambient that it doesn't warrant an actual projection of light by the game's engine. It is mostly a suggested light source, but doesn't actually shine real light.\par
\par
\pard\sl276\slmult1\par
\ul\b Art Tips: Large objects - Sketching/Measurement/Structuring\ulnone\b0\par
\par
Althought small objects already are sort of sketched through blobbing the silhouette, things of larger size may benefit from proper sketch lines, guiding lines and measuring lines.\par
\par
These are done on a seperate, disposable layer which mimics how traditional pencil artists actually draw art, where they draw wireframes, basic 3D shapes and lines to help them measure, estimate and guess how they should draw something before they actually draw the true lines that'll be the final ones.\par
\par
There's plenty of youtube video tutorials by people like Proko, Sycra, FZD Studio, and many others that show fundamentals of drawing. Its basically about applying that to the initial stages of pixel art.\par
\pard\sl240\slmult1\par
\par
\ul\b Art Tips: Additional miscellanous tips\ulnone\b0\par
\par
\b * Critical gameplay objects (player characters) vs Background Items:\b0\par
When playing a 2D video game its comfortable to the player to have good visual clarity what belongs to the background and isn't relevant to the action. \par
\par
So things like backgrounds or background props/decorations should be desaturated and have less contrast, making them more dull and flat compared to active characters on the screen, projectiles, items and other active foreground props, which pop out more by having stronger color saturation and stronger contrast (darker darks and brighter brights).\par
\par
So when coloring objects for game-use, its a good idea to clamp certain ranges of color saturation and contrast/brightness, for objects you know will be used for background or foreground purposes.\par
\par
\par
\b * PSRC's Hybrid Cartoon-Realistic Style with -20% Size Reduction:\b0\par
Game engines that deal with physics or collisions work better when objects are not thin. \par
\par
An example of this is a fast moving projectile that tries to collide with a thin object but unexpected doesn't register the hit and just flies past it like it wasn't there; this is a common issue and there are workaround to fix it, but it is easier to make game objects more chunky/compact/thick and to restrict maximum velocities of moving things so that objects are guaranteed to collide properly with each other.\par
\par
Besides that, controlling a character with cartoony proportions is often more fun and easier to see properly, since hyperrealistic measurements may not always lead to more enjoyable gameplay or visual style. Its the reason why the head is so big on the PSRC Human Mk2 character so that I could fully fit a full suite of facial details on it within the tiny pixel art constraints that I'm working with.\par
\par
\par
\b * Using Textures to add "teeth/grip" to surfaces or skip tedious work:\b0\par
When drawing things with large surfaces and monotone details, you can skip some of the work during shading or modelling by using a seamlessly tiling texture.\par
\par
Very useful for things like leaves on a tree or bush, wood surface on wooden boxes, dents on a meteor, or scratches/rust on a metal panel.\par
\par
Create a new layer, set it to overlay mode, tweak the layer opacity as needed, use the flood fill tool with an existing pixel art seamless repeating texture image (like those used on Minecraft blocks, except greyscale) as the paint. This combined with a magic wand selection tool or similar will instantly let you fill in the areas you want with the texture from that image source as a repeating pattern. \par
\par
This technique can save you a lot of time than trying to shade each individual leaf by hand.\par
\par
\par
\b * Overlaying decals/complex forms over simple shapes:\b0\par
One cool trick is to create smaller reusable decals that you can then copy-paste or use a clone brush or use an overlay mode layer to apply to other objects to insert elements, shapes, patterns or symbols onto larger objects without having to redraw them manually each time.\par
\par
\par
\b * My personal thinking process when I draw PSRC sprites:\b0\par
\par
When drawing from reference, I first outline the size of the object by measuring the dimensions (lenght & height) according to the 32px/1m standard. \par
\par
Then, I proceed to think of the process of drawing from the reference as if I'm doing the activity of Rock Wall Climbing as a metaphor. I look for easy nooks/anchors to place my "hand" or "foot" in so I can climb higher towards my destination.\par
\par
Translated to pixel art drawing, I look for the easiest "prominent/remarkable, yet simple shapes" that I can most easiest focus on and I will draw the object by first drawing these "anchors", then bridging the gap between the two. This method/thinking is especially useful when drawing complex machinery with many components.\par
\par
I use the 5 colors freely, that is, grey, light grey and dark grey to define structures, black for holes where light does not shine and white for light emissions or writings, markings, symbols. \par
\par
I don't use a single color to draw something unless its really simple. Most things are too hard for my mind to comprehend and keep track of what I'm doing due to lack of seperation of sectors in the object during initial drawing when I don't have any shading yet.\par
\par
I also utilize 4 primary colors (red, blue, green, yellow) to temporarily mark important parts to better distinguish the shape during the initial structuring of the object. Later, I convert them to greys as mentioned above. I do this if it feels too complex to handle with only greys. Its a useful handicap/crutch to get through tough parts.\par
\par
I begin drawing by using rough blobs or approximate rough sketch lines to define the shape of the object I want to draw. This helps my mind to picture the scope and complexity of the object more clearly, to give me some peace of mind and offload the amount of data I need to keep at once in my head.\par
\par
I prefer to start with the part that is both easiest to draw, but also most significant or important to that object, like wheels on a car or bike or a head on a human or animal. I then draw the rest based on that while also adjusting measurements of both the starting piece and the other things around it to make sure I'm not going off track too much.\par
\par
For the canvas, I either pick a lime green/purple color, or a soft desatured matte color that suits the mood, to both make the grey object stand out, but also not burn my eyes/retinas or make me squint to comprehend the image due to bg and object being too blended together.\par
\par
After getting the basic proportions and structure down, I focus on gradually bringing out the details out of the reference image as much as possible, but making concessions where there are just not enough pixels to fit something in. While doing it, I compare the subject I'm drawing with a floating human sprite template on a seperate layer that I can freely move around whenever I want to. This little trick helps me see and adjust proportions and sizes to be more sensible and useful, since usually i'll be mistaken in the first pass anyway. I rarely get it right the first time, its always an iterative process that starts messy and becomes better over time.\par
\par
Lastly, I may go look up a tutorial or a cheat sheet on how I should render the texture or material of the object to make it look right according the standard 'top-right-front lighting' + 'ambient omni light'. This is where I do the shading to bring out the correct level of shininess or matte or smoothness or roughness for each part of the object.\par
\par
